    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d608">

                <pb facs="0656" n="644" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg644"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/8221: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Athens</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-02-08T17:00:00+02:00">February 8, 1941—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> Received February 9—4:33 p.m.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>63. Telegrams Nos. 53 and 54 of February 7 from Sofia.<anchor n="16"
                        xml:id="d608-f1"/> Both the Brazilian Minister<anchor n="16a"
                        xml:id="d608-f2"/> and the Political Director of the Foreign Office told me
                    this morning that they have no definite information to justify immediate alarm
                    but they are nevertheless apprehensive. According to the Brazilian Minister an
                    attack on Bulgaria by Turkey in the event of a German drive in this direction is
                    still only a matter of probability, and he expressed the hope that Colonel
                    Donovan may have been able to stiffen the latter’s attitude. In this connection,
                    however, the Turkish Military Attaché told Major [Baker?] today that Turkey now
                    feels herself menaced by both Germany and Russia which again has a considerable
                    force on the Caucasus front. It is his idea that Germany will start advancing
                    into Bulgaria soon, and that the Bulgarian forces will then be used to block any
                    Turkish move while the Germans take Salonika and probably all Greece. On the
                    other hand, some other competent observers here, including the Under Secretary
                    for Foreign Affairs<anchor n="16b" xml:id="d608-f3"/> with whom I recently had a
                    long conversation on this matter, believe it still possible that the Germans
                    intend only to establish a military control in Bulgaria similar to that which
                    they now enjoy in Rumania, to make the establishment of an eastern front by the
                    British impracticable, and to preserve Balkan economy for themselves intact.</p>
                <p>Repeat to Ankara and Sofia.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
